{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And He said further, etc. So shall you say, etc.</b> The reason He repeated a second time the Name YHWH, may He be blessed, and was not satisfied with informing him of the name Ehyeh, is that the Name YHWH encompasses all the holy aspects, the Unique and Unified One, may He be blessed. At first He mentioned to him only one aspect of the holy aspects by which HaShem is designated when Israel is in distress, and in his time — when they were in distress — for this reason He mentioned the name Ehyeh, appropriate to the circumstance, and He mentioned the unified Name." "A further reason that He repeated to say ״So shall you say, etc. YHWH,״ etc. — is because El Elyon was concerned that Moses might understand that HaShem had withheld from him the authority to say to Israel the Name YHWH, and had also withheld saying what He said nearby: ״the God of, etc., has sent me,״ etc., and that he would say only ״Ehyeh has sent me,״ etc. Therefore He repeated to say that he should say also <b>YHWH, the God of your fathers,</b> etc. And it appears to me that in this utterance He also alluded to the name Ehyeh at the beginning of the names of the patriarchs: the aleph of Abraham and the two yods — one in Isaac and one in Jacob:" "<b>Further,</b> HaShem intends that Israel should know that He is the One who promised the patriarchs the promise of redemption, for the name Ehyeh was not brought in the earlier verses that appear in the promises. Also, because He would yet cause them to hear His holy declarations and would open His word by saying ״I am YHWH your God,״ therefore He prefaced by informing them of the name Ehyeh and the Name YHWH — the name Ehyeh alluded to in ״Anokhi,״ and the Name YHWH in its plain sense — so that they would validate the words of Moses when HaShem would speak and say ״I am YHWH,״ and understand. We thus find ourselves saying that Moses needed to say to Israel in this order: ״Ehyeh has sent me to you,״ and since this is a name new to their knowledge and is called after the event — meaning, that He was with them in their distress — he would repeat and say to them: <b>the God of your fathers.</b> And perhaps He also alluded to three letters inscribed in the names of their patriarchs, which is the name Ehyeh suspended above their heads from on high."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :register {:flags {1 ["God"], 2 ["God"]}}, :notes {2 "In the phrase ה׳ שידעו ישראל (opening comment [2]), I read ה׳ as HaShem (the speaker) rather than as an interrogative; the context — HaShem intending Israel to know — supports this reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:11.475889155Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}